README
======

GIMP 3 VERSION
--------------

This fork carries the port of the wavelet denoise plugin to GIMP 3. The
original plugin by Marco Rossini is at
https://github.com/mrossini-ethz/gimp-wavelet-denoise and supports GIMP 2.x.
The port is being offered upstream; until it is merged, this is where it lives.

What is new in the GIMP 3 version (see ChangeLog for details):

- Runs in GIMP 3.0 and newer.
- 16-bit and floating point images are denoised at full precision. Floating
  point images keep values outside of [0,1].
- All settings can be passed from scripts, and GIMP remembers the last used
  ones.
- Several bug fixes.

For building and installing see the file INSTALL. Please report problems with
the GIMP 3 version at
https://github.com/sandbranch/gimp-wavelet-denoise/issues

The wavelet denoise plugin for The GIMP is an algorithm copied and slightly
altered from the UFRaw program (which inherited the algorithm from dcraw).
Instead of denoising all RGB channels at once the plugin implementation allows
to denoise the RGB channels individually and - even more useful - to denoise
the YCbCr or CIELAB channels individually. The colour model conversions are
nearly lossless as the internal calculations are done in floating point numbers
and rounding errors are avoided.

LICENCE
-------

Copyright (C) 2008 by Marco Rossini. Distributed under the General Public
License. See the file COPYING which contains the license.

INSTALLATION
------------

See the file INSTALL for instructions how to install the plugins.

USAGE
-----

Once the plugin is installed successfully, the plugin can be found in The GIMP
using the menu "Filters->Enhance->Wavelet denoise". It works for both Grayscale
and RGB (full colour) images, including alpha channels, in any precision (8 and
16 bit integer as well as floating point images). The plugin dialog allows to
adjust several parameters.

1. First, the choice has to be made over what colour model is to be used. There
   are three possibilities (maybe there will be more in the future):

   a) RGB     (red-green-blue)

   b) YCbCr   (luminance-blueness-redness)

   c) CIELAB  (lightness-chroma)

   YCbCr and CIELAB allow separate reduction of luminance and chroma (colour)
   noise.

2. The preview channel can be selected. Either all channels are displayed or
   a single channel, in grayscale or (with "Show in color") in colour. Hold
   the "Hold to compare" button to see the displayed channels without
   denoising.

3. Each channel has its own sliders. The threshold sets how strongly the
   channel is denoised, 0 leaves it untouched. The softness controls the
   softness of the thresholding. The greater the softness, the more noise
   remains in the image.

For camera pictures I advise to use YCbCr mode (which is nearly lossless) and
to at least denoise the Cb and Cr channels to reduce the chroma (colour) noise.
Cameras shooting in JPEG mode normally have chroma noise already reduced. If
desired, the luminance noise can be reduced. This channel usually contains
most of the fine structures in the image and should mostly be left alone.

SCRIPTING
---------

The plugin can be called from scripts as "plug-in-wavelet-denoise". Besides
the image and drawable it takes the color model ("ycbcr", "lab" or "rgb") and
a threshold (0 to 10) and softness (0 to 1) for each channel: "threshold-1",
"threshold-2", "threshold-3", "threshold-alpha" and the matching "softness-"
arguments. The first channel is Y, L* or R (or gray for grayscale images), the
second Cb, a* or G, the third Cr, b* or B.
